  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are the four main compass points?",
      "a": "North, east, south and west. They are called the cardinal points, and the four points in between are north-east, south-east, south-west and north-west.",
      "asks": [
        "What are cardinal directions?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a compass point north?",
      "a": "A compass needle is a tiny magnet. The Earth acts like a giant magnet too, so the needle lines up with it and points towards magnetic north, which is close to the North Pole.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a compass work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does the sun rise?",
      "a": "The sun rises roughly in the east and sets roughly in the west, everywhere on Earth. That is because the Earth spins from west to east.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the sun rise in the east?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is between north and east?",
      "a": "North-east. The four in-between points are north-east, south-east, south-west and north-west, and their names always start with north or south.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the eight compass points?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can I remember the compass points?",
      "a": "Go clockwise from the top and say Never Eat Soggy Waffles: north, east, south, west. Some children say Naughty Elephants Squirt Water instead.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a trick for compass directions?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "North, east, south and west are called the cardinal points.",
    "A magnetic compass points to magnetic north, which is not exactly at the North Pole and slowly moves over the years.",
    "The sun rises exactly due east only around the equinoxes in March and September."
  ],
